---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { worktreeComparison } from "../data/site";

const rows = [
  ["Project files", "Separate checkout, shared Git repository", "Copied workspace, no host folders mounted"],
  ["Command execution", "On your Mac, with whatever permissions the agent already has", "In a Linux microVM with its own kernel"],
  ["Outbound network", "Uses your existing host network policy", "Offline by default"],
  ["How changes come back", "Already on disk in the linked checkout", "Review and apply an exported Git patch"],
  ["Lifetime", "Persists until you remove it", "Fixed deadline from creation"],
];

const commands = [
  "git worktree add -b fix/ci ../ci-fix",
  "dynocap doctor --fix ../ci-fix --runtime shell:1",
  "dynocap clone ../ci-fix ci-fix --runtime shell:1 --history head",
  "dynocap shell ci-fix",
  "dynocap exec ci-fix -- bash scripts/check.sh",
  "dynocap diff ci-fix",
  "dynocap finish ci-fix",
].join("\n");
---

<Base
  title={worktreeComparison.title}
  description={worktreeComparison.description}
>
  <article class="band">
    <header class="hero">
      <div>
        <h1 id="content" tabindex="-1">{worktreeComparison.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A Git worktree gives your coding agent a separate checkout. DynoCap
          gives its commands a separate Linux machine on your Mac. Use a worktree
          for parallel edits, DynoCap for isolated execution, or both for a fix
          you want to test in Linux and land on a branch.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="seal-link hero__cta" href="#use-both">
            <span>See the combined workflow</span>
            <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </a>
          <a class="seal-link" href="/#install">
            <span>Get started with DynoCap</span>
            <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="choice">
        <div>
          <dt>Git worktree</dt>
          <dd>Give each agent its own branch and working directory.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>DynoCap</dt>
          <dd>Give the code it runs a disposable, offline-by-default Linux VM.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
      <h2 id="compare-title">What each tool isolates</h2>
      <table aria-labelledby="compare-title">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Feature</th>
            <th scope="col">Git worktree</th>
            <th scope="col">DynoCap task</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(([dimension, worktree, sandbox]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{dimension}</th>
              <td>{worktree}</td>
              <td>{sandbox}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="note references">
        <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree">Git worktree reference</a>
        <a href="/docs/explanation/security-and-isolation">DynoCap isolation boundary</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="daily-title">
      <h2 id="daily-title">When to use each with a coding agent</h2>
      <dl class="situations">
        <div>
          <dt>Two agents editing a repo you trust</dt>
          <dd>
            Use a worktree per branch for tasks like adding tests and updating
            docs in parallel. Keep your normal editor, local tooling, and Git
            workflow.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A PR ships a repro script you want to try</dt>
          <dd>
            Run the script in a DynoCap task. It works on a project copy in an
            offline guest, with no host directories mounted. If the project
            needs packages, you can
            <a href="/docs/how-to/manage-dependencies-and-caching">prepare a dependency cache</a>
            for offline installs.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>CI fails on Linux and passes on macOS</dt>
          <dd>
            Run the failing command in DynoCap to investigate Linux-specific
            shell or tooling behavior. Use a worktree for the fix and bring
            the reviewed patch back when the guest test passes.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="use-both" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="workflow-title">Keep the branch. Sandbox the commands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          From the main checkout, create a worktree and copy it into DynoCap.
          This example assumes an installed DynoCap CLI and a project with a
          Bash-only check at <code>scripts/check.sh</code>. Use the guest shell
          to investigate and edit, then exit it before running the check.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="command">
        <p class="command-bar">Run one line at a time</p>
        <pre><code>{commands}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Run <code>finish</code> before the task expires. It exports the patch and
        logs, then tears down the VM. Review the patch and run <code>git apply --check</code>
        with the printed patch path from inside <code>../ci-fix</code>. Then apply it
        there and commit as usual.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        If your coding agent runs on your Mac, give it the
        <a href="/docs/how-to/configure-mcp-server">DynoCap MCP tools</a>
        to run commands in the guest. Commands issued through its regular host
        shell still run on your Mac.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="seal-link hero__cta" href="/docs/tutorials/agent-task-workflow">
          <span>Follow the agent task tutorial</span>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero h1 {
    max-width: 17ch;
  }

  .choice {
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--c-accent);
    padding-inline-start: var(--gutter);
  }

  .choice > div {
    padding-block: 1.25rem;
  }

  .choice > div + div {
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--c-line);
  }

  .choice dt,
  .command-bar,
  thead th {
    font: var(--t-xs) / 1.5 var(--f-mono);
  }

  .choice dd {
    max-width: 27ch;
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
    font-size: var(--t-m);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    table-layout: fixed;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  thead th:first-child {
    width: 23%;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: clamp(0.5rem, 1.5vw, 1.25rem);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--c-line);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  tbody td,
  .situations dd,
  .command-bar {
    color: var(--c-muted);
  }

  .references {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .situations > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
    gap: 0.5rem var(--gutter);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--c-line);
    padding-block: 1.25rem;
  }

  .situations dt {
    font-size: var(--t-m);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .situations dd {
    max-width: 54ch;
  }

  .note a,
  .situations a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .note a:hover,
  .situations a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--c-accent);
  }

  .command {
    border: 1px solid var(--c-line);
  }

  .command-bar {
    padding: 0.75rem var(--gutter);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--c-line);
  }

  pre {
    padding: 1rem var(--gutter);
    font: var(--t-s) / 1.65 var(--f-mono);
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  #use-both .actions {
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .situations > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    table {
      font-size: var(--t-s);
    }

    thead th:first-child {
      width: 28%;
    }
  }
</style>
